{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The Rock, His work is perfect.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the Holy One, blessed be He, is called צור (Rock) from the language of strength, as it is written (Isaiah 51:1): ״הביטו אל צור חוצבתם״. For just as the rock (צור) is strong and is the foundation of the earth, so the Holy One, blessed be He, is the foundation of the world. And Moses now begins to speak to the witnesses and says: this Lord whom I wish to speak of is a צור (Rock) and strong, than whom there is none like Him; yet He is תמים (perfect) — He does not deal with His creatures by force, but rather תמים פעלו, His work is perfect: with perfection and humility He conducts Himself with His creatures. And with this expression David, of blessed memory, praised Him (II Samuel 22:31): ״האל תמים דרכו״, that is, even though He is אל (a mighty one), His attribute with His creatures is to conduct Himself with them in perfection. And this is in the manner of what is written (Job 37:23): ״שדי לא מצאנוהו שגיא כח״, that is, even though He is Shaddai and is great in power, we do not find Him great in power, but rather perfect and abundantly forgiving. And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: every place where you find the might of the Holy One, blessed be He, you find His humility. And this matter is mentioned in the Torah, repeated in the Prophets, and triplicated in the Writings. He said, תמים פעלו — His work is perfect — for He is not like the other forces, whose action is deficient, since they must receive power from what is above them, and that power needs another power, and so from power to power, up to the supreme power, which is HaShem of all, blessed be He, who needs none other; rather, all the powers receive from Him. Therefore תמים פעלו — all His deeds are perfect." "<b>And I heard</b> from the mouth of my teacher, R. Shemuel, may his lamp shine: that there is no need to say in the blessing of the moon 'פועל אמת' — 'whose action is truth' — because the action of one who needs to receive from what is above him is not truth, that is, it is not תמימה (perfect) and is not at the level of perfection. And there is no deed at the level of perfection except His deed, may He be exalted; and therefore he said that it is fitting to bless Him thus: 'פועל אמת שפעולתו אמת' — 'the Agent of truth, whose action is truth' — and concerning the moon he said that it be renewed, returning to the Creator, may He be exalted, who is 'פועל אמת ופעולתו אמת,' in the manner that he said here: תמים פעלו." "<b>And it appears to me</b> to interpret הצור תמים פעלו as follows: that Moses alluded here to the formation of the first man, and to his sin and his punishment. The matter is because he was the cause of the decree of death upon all his descendants, and therefore Moses needed to mention this on the day of his death. And the reason he called Him הצור (the Rock) is because He צר (formed) all the forms, all of them, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded (I Samuel 2:2): ״אין צור כאלהינו״ — there is no צור (rock) like our Elohim — meaning, there is no צייר (painter/former) like our Elohim. And so (Isaiah 26:4): ״צור עולמים״ — He formed (צר) two worlds. And it is written concerning the first man (Genesis 2:7): ״וייצר ה׳ אלהים את האדם״. And the sense of the verse is: the צור, when He formed the first man, who was His deed and the work of His hands — His intention for him was that he be תמים, perfect, serving HaShem on earth like an angel in heaven. But because he sinned, He punished him with death, and this in justice — this is the meaning of כי כל דרכיו משפט, for all His ways are justice. For He is אל אמונה, a God of faithfulness — that is, the generations pass away and He endures, and there is no perversion in His judgment, for צדיק וישר הוא, righteous and upright is He. And the verse that follows it explains and clarifies his sin, which is what it says: שחת לו — 'he corrupted to Him' — and it did not say להם (to them). The intention is that the first man corrupted himself לו — to his Father who is in heaven. And this corruption is what you know: that he cut down the plantings, and this is the sin of the makers of the Calf, concerning whom He used this very expression (Exodus 32:7): שחת עמך, even though the sin is different and it was not the case that the Father and the sons were cut down in one place. And Scripture further clarified what the sin and this corruption of the first man was, and this is what it says: לא — that is, by the word לא (no). And this is that he transgressed a לא תעשה (negative commandment) in His saying (Genesis 2:17): ״ומעץ הדעת טוב ורע לא תאכל ממנו״ — this is what it says: שחת לו לא. And it says: בניו מומם — 'his sons, their blemish' — for his sons, Cain and Abel, corrupted themselves. And you already know that this one was killed and that one was cut off; and the corruption they did was their מומם, a great blemish for them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :register {:fixes 1, :flags {0 ["Lord"], 2 ["God"]}}, :notes {1 "Hebrew 'נר\"ו' resolved as 'may his lamp shine' (נרו יאיר), a common honorific for a living teacher; reading uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:20.544023636Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}